PR #699  overall: potential_risk
Spec: IOMMU GPA superpages overlapping virtual interrupt file regions grant devices unintended access to MSI configuration space; fix by reducing effective page size to isolate the interrupt file from the superpage mapping.
  [RISK] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:389-399  imsic_cold_irqchip_init
      IMSIC interrupt-file pages are correctly registered into the root domain at 4KB granularity (IMSIC_MMIO_PAGE_SZ) with SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_MMIO flag. However, no IOMMU page-table constraint is derived from this registration — the firmware trusts the hypervisor to program the IOMMU without awareness of these protected sub-regions. If the hypervisor creates a superpage GPA mapping overlapping an IMSIC interrupt file page, the domain system provides no enforcement mechanism to prevent or detect it.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:735-752  root_add_memregion
      The merge logic coalesces adjacent domain memory regions with matching order and flags by incrementing order by 1 — the functional equivalent of superpage coalescing. The condition checks base alignment (nreg->base & (BIT(nreg->order + 1) - 1)), contiguity, order equality, and flag equality. Critically, it does NOT check whether the merged region would span a separately-registered protected sub-region (e.g., an IMSIC interrupt file registered with different flags). In the current codebase, the flags-equality check (nreg->flags == nreg1->flags) prevents merging MMIO regions with non-MMIO regions, so a pure-MMIO large region won't silently absorb a non-MMIO page. However, the invariant that 'all adjacent same-flag regions are safe to merge' is implicit and not enforced by any cross-check against a finer-grained region table.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:95-122  sbi_domain_memregion_init
      Rounds an address range up to the smallest enclosing power-of-2 size (stored as 'order'). This is the semantic equivalent of determining a page size for mapping. The function itself has no logic to detect whether the power-of-2 region crosses an MMIO/interrupt-file boundary. In the actual call chain, this function is invoked from sbi_domain_root_add_memrange() which first splits the range at alignment boundaries (align=IMSIC_MMIO_PAGE_SZ=4KB for IMSIC registrations), so the ranges passed in are already bounded at 4KB - meaning the rounding won't cross MMIO boundaries in practice. However, this safety depends entirely on the caller's splitting logic; the function itself provides no guard against producing an order that spans heterogeneous regions.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:497-526  sbi_domain_check_addr_range
      This function implements the EXACT correct algorithmic pattern for detecting cross-region overlap: it walks an address range by finding the next subset-region boundary at each step via find_next_subset_region(), effectively splitting the range at finer-grained region boundaries. At each step it calls sbi_domain_check_addr() which has MMIO flag awareness. This proves OpenSBI already has the primitive needed for IOMMU page-table constraint enforcement. However, the function only returns a boolean (access allowed/denied) and is NOT wired to IOMMU page-table constraint derivation — it cannot emit 'this range must be split at address X because a protected sub-region starts there.' The algorithm exists but produces only a yes/no answer instead of a split plan.
  [OK] platform/generic/andes/andes_pma.c:89-112  has_pma_region_overlap
      Implements the standard interval-overlap predicate (MAX(startA, startB) <= MIN(endA, endB)) for detecting memory region overlaps. This is the correct detection primitive. However, it is confined to Andes PMA programming at the physical address level — it checks overlap against hardware PMA regions for a single vendor's platform init, not generalized to IOMMU GPA-to-MMIO overlap detection. The scope limitation is by design, not a bug. The function correctly serves its specific purpose.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:445-464  sanitize_domain
      Removes 'covered' regions: if region A is a subset of region B with identical flags (checked via is_region_compatible which verifies both subset relation AND flag equality), region A is deleted. This implements 'coarser region wins' semantics. In the IOMMU scenario, this is the INVERSE of the needed behavior — a large GPA superpage spanning a protected interrupt file should be split around the interrupt file, not have the interrupt file's registration removed. Currently this is safe because the flags-equality guard prevents a large non-MMIO region from removing a small MMIO region — but if a large MMIO region covers a small MMIO interrupt file page (both carrying SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_MMIO), the interrupt file's fine-grained registration would be silently deleted, losing the information that a 4KB sub-page needs isolation.
